Kubernetes Deployment Learning Notes
Project Name

AI Memory Router using FastAPI, Docker & Kubernetes

Goal

The final goal is to deploy our AI Memory Router inside a Kubernetes cluster so that users can access it through HTTP requests.

Final Architecture:

User
   │
Browser/Postman
   │
HTTP Request
   │
Kubernetes Service
   │
FastAPI Application
   │
AI Memory Router
   │
├── RAG Backend
├── Graph Backend
└── Tabular Backend
   │
Groq API
Why can't we deploy main.py directly?

Our original project works like this:

python main.py

Execution flow:

Program Starts
      │
Reads predefined queries
      │
Calls Groq
      │
Prints output
      │
Program Ends

This is called a console (terminal) application.

The problem is that Kubernetes expects applications to keep running and wait for client requests.

Why do we need an API?

Instead of running the program once and exiting, we want it to behave like this:

Application Starts
      │
Waits for Requests
      │
User Sends Query
      │
Processes Query
      │
Returns Response
      │
Waits for Next User

This is exactly what a REST API does.

Why FastAPI?

FastAPI is a Python framework used to build REST APIs.

It acts as the communication layer between the user and our AI application.

Instead of:

User
   │
main.py

we now have:

User
   │
FastAPI
   │
process_query()

FastAPI receives HTTP requests, calls our AI logic, and returns JSON responses.

Why did we create process_query()?

Originally, our AI logic was inside run().

run(query)

run() only prints the answer to the terminal.

FastAPI cannot use printed output.

So we created:

process_query(query)

Instead of printing, it returns:

memory type
retrieved context
AI answer

This makes the logic reusable by any application (FastAPI today, other interfaces in the future).

Why did we create api.py?

api.py is the entry point for our web service.

Responsibilities:

Start FastAPI.
Define API endpoints.
Accept user requests.
Call process_query().
Return JSON.

We kept the AI logic in main.py and the web layer in api.py. This separation makes the project cleaner and easier to maintain.

API Endpoints
GET /

Purpose:

Checks whether the server is running.

Response:

{
  "message": "Welcome to the AI Memory Router API!"
}
POST /ask

Purpose:

Accepts a user query.

Example Request:

{
    "query":"What did user 42 order?"
}

Example Response:

{
    "memory_type":"tabular",
    "context":"...",
    "answer":"..."
}
What is JSON?

JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is the standard format used to exchange data between clients and servers.

Example:

{
    "query":"Hello"
}

FastAPI automatically converts this into a Python object.

What is Swagger UI?

FastAPI automatically generates interactive API documentation.

URL:

http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs

Advantages:

Test APIs without Postman.
View all available endpoints.
See request and response formats.
What did we test?

We verified that:

FastAPI starts successfully.
The GET endpoint works.
The POST endpoint accepts JSON.
process_query() is called.
The router selects the correct backend.
Groq generates an answer.
The response is returned as JSON.

This confirmed that our AI application now behaves like a web service.

